## Proof

We are given
$$\Phi(z,\bar z)=w(z)\exp\!\Big[\psi(z,\bar z)\Big],\qquad \psi(z,\bar z):=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\iint_D \frac{A(\zeta,\bar\zeta)}{\zeta-z}\,d\zeta\wedge d\bar\zeta,$$
where $w(z)$ is holomorphic in $D$ (hence $\partial w/\partial\bar z=0$), and the distributional identity
$$\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar z}(z-\alpha)^{-1}=-2\pi i\,\delta(z-\alpha),$$
with $\delta(z-\alpha)$ normalized so that $\iint f(\zeta)\,\delta(z-\zeta)\,d\zeta\wedge d\bar\zeta = f(z)$ (consistent with the identity, since $d\zeta\wedge d\bar\zeta=-2i\,d\xi\,d\eta$ and the classical fundamental solution $\partial_{\bar z}(1/z)=\pi\delta^{(2)}$).

**Goal.** Show $\dfrac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\bar z}=A(z,\bar z)\,\Phi$ in $D$.

---

### Step 1. Reduce to the exponent

Since $\Phi = w\,e^{\psi}$ and $\partial w/\partial\bar z=0$,
$$\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\bar z}=w\,e^{\psi}\,\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\bar z}=\Phi\,\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\bar z}.$$
Thus it suffices to prove
$$\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\bar z}=A(z,\bar z)\qquad(z\in D).$$

### Step 2. Differentiate under the integral sign

For $z\in D$ (interior point), we may pass $\partial/\partial\bar z$ under the integral in the distributional sense:
$$\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\bar z}=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\iint_D A(\zeta,\bar\zeta)\,\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar z}\!\left[(\zeta-z)^{-1}\right]d\zeta\wedge d\bar\zeta.$$

### Step 3. Apply the given identity

Observe $(\zeta-z)^{-1}=-(z-\zeta)^{-1}$. Setting $\alpha=\zeta$ in the provided identity,
$$\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar z}(z-\zeta)^{-1}=-2\pi i\,\delta(z-\zeta),$$
so
$$\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar z}(\zeta-z)^{-1}=-\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar z}(z-\zeta)^{-1}=2\pi i\,\delta(z-\zeta).$$

### Step 4. Evaluate the integral

Substituting back,
$$\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\bar z}=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\iint_D A(\zeta,\bar\zeta)\cdot 2\pi i\,\delta(z-\zeta)\,d\zeta\wedge d\bar\zeta=\iint_D A(\zeta,\bar\zeta)\,\delta(z-\zeta)\,d\zeta\wedge d\bar\zeta.$$

By the defining property of $\delta(z-\zeta)$, the integral collapses to the value of the integrand at $\zeta=z$. For $z\in D$ this point lies inside the domain of integration, giving
$$\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\bar z}=A(z,\bar z).$$

### Step 5. Conclude

Therefore
$$\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\bar z}=\Phi\,\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\bar z}=A(z,\bar z)\,\Phi\qquad\text{in }D,$$
which is precisely the equation $\dfrac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\bar z}=A(z,\bar z)\,\Phi$ to be verified. $\blacksquare$

